{"rowToken":"r_657b0489d99fafc89f06dcf076f6","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which items belong in the postoperative day 3 SBAR handoff to home health, and that tea preference is not transition-critical.","stageEvaluations":{"s_25598546bd98bb08b5e91cb269ba":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_0e031733a0a1cae0d34e1558b741","e_1ba27683c483c1a5362433ec4cc7"],"basis":"Postoperative day 1 supplies the creatinine rise to 1.5 mg/dL and the barrier list (lives alone, walk-up, uncertain rivaroxaban adherence) behind keyed options B, C, and D."},"s_69357df2450070b55e6d5d64445b":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_33927050fa08837fea02bc533dd0"],"basis":"Postoperative day 2 establishes the son's limited availability and phone-contact role, the walker/raised seat/reacher equipment, and PT clearance underlying option F."},"s_5e43e8137907d4f4e4d9ac684cb3":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_35864be68d090e3c8f9506b8c26d","e_16880b4e1910d0092b2afefe6cb5"],"basis":"The day 3 note itemizes the SBAR content the nurse already transmitted, matching keyed options A, B, C, D, and F almost phrase for phrase."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_83cc6fdb02748ecbdf9614fb6204","e_88df418ebeb8361bebd7eb70ab19","e_1dc86c340096098dcc3013b4e472","e_7722ecda174c31bfa9ec76f0fbe5"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_0e031733a0a1cae0d34e1558b741","e_1ba27683c483c1a5362433ec4cc7","e_33927050fa08837fea02bc533dd0","e_35864be68d090e3c8f9506b8c26d","e_16880b4e1910d0092b2afefe6cb5"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_5e43e8137907d4f4e4d9ac684cb3","evidenceIds":["e_35864be68d090e3c8f9506b8c26d","e_16880b4e1910d0092b2afefe6cb5"],"verbatimSpan":"communicates language needs, the need for an interpreter for teaching, social situation, functional status, equipment, rivaroxaban dose/duration and adherence concerns, renal-function trend, bleeding surveillance, son's role as the communication contact, and the confirmed follow-up appointment"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The day 3 exhibit narrates the completed SBAR call and lists language needs, interpreter, social situation, functional status, equipment, rivaroxaban dose/duration and adherence, renal trend, bleeding surveillance, son as contact, and follow-up, which is the keyed set. A candidate can copy the answer instead of judging what changes the receiving nurse's monitoring. The Chinese exhibit carries the same enumeration."}
{"rowToken":"r_bf649adac7d3a600e30061bb119d","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"Whether metformin should be held because eGFR 33 has not returned to the client's baseline 42 and remains in the caution range.","stageEvaluations":{"s_98c3a973d59ec06850741ce96180":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_9d91ca911fbd03ee34b2e967c401"],"basis":"The 8-hour update covers the positive C. difficile PCR, potassium 3.1 replacement, and the family's missing gown and gloves, none of which bear on resuming metformin."},"s_ca4a63b7c3796350548c085e1f42":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_b2d70c74262bd4cdb53a31f30887"],"basis":"The 20-hour creatinine of 1.9 and worsening perianal maceration show interim recovery but never state whether renal clearance is adequate for metformin."},"s_6cc89845853d79b21350308f3d54":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_0259cb8015d7d6fff978c8a7b102","e_b02f51baf2f2fefefdff8aa96a15"],"basis":"Stage 3 carries the provider's request to assess metformin restart plus creatinine 1.6 and eGFR 33, the values the hold decision rests on."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_eec944e77f61ab86707951ded1fc","e_bf612441ff404df52524449e207f","e_90267e22242e0963bdab0eeca5b3","e_d8b5b5f8231d93ef691612eed4db"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_9d91ca911fbd03ee34b2e967c401","e_b2d70c74262bd4cdb53a31f30887","e_0259cb8015d7d6fff978c8a7b102","e_b02f51baf2f2fefefdff8aa96a15"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"No stage states that metformin should be held, resumed, or is contraindicated; the judgment comes from comparing eGFR 33 with the baseline 42 already supplied in the stem. Potassium replacement, PPE coaching, and wound care in the earlier stages are unrelated to lactic-acidosis risk. Both language versions carry the same renal values."}
{"rowToken":"r_94d23f6c439fda25e6ab445cfa92","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"Whether the nurse defers the client's return to long-haul driving pending provider clearance rather than reassuring him or calling it an employer matter.","stageEvaluations":{"s_052e75edc6951d7909f90842a32a":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034"],"basis":"Stage 1's ibuprofen request and the wife's frozen spinach purchase drive other parts and say nothing about resuming commercial truck driving."},"s_9bac0f616d7bd3787076166f67d9":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5"],"basis":"Stage 2 confirms the provider judged INR 1.9 subtherapeutic and that the home enoxaparin bridge continues, establishing the dual-anticoagulation risk behind deferring driving."},"s_0ec5ca15f74b0296e396e4afbc1c":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"basis":"Stage 3 contains the client's statement that he is scheduled to drive his truck in four days, the request the item asks the nurse to answer."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_063aa99c7f1597f729021f722d89","e_f65e76efa97a9fced722afd333b0","e_168a69dab185d980f9567b0abf3a","e_2aa938e2b9f0a4decd4468c2ea56"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034","e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5","e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The record never says driving is unsafe, never mentions provider clearance, and never rejects the stretch-every-two-hours or employer-referral options; the candidate must infer immobility and bleeding risk from the ongoing bridge with an unstable INR. Stage 1's NSAID and diet content does not touch occupational safety. English and Chinese state the driving question identically."}
{"rowToken":"r_c86be88bf736e683b9dd9029682e","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"Whether sudden headache with confusion and coffee-ground emesis warrant 911 while disproportionate bruising and a recurrent pressure-controlled nosebleed warrant same-day clinic contact.","stageEvaluations":{"s_052e75edc6951d7909f90842a32a":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034"],"basis":"Stage 1's reconciliation list with ibuprofen raises general bleeding risk but never ranks any specific bleeding sign by urgency."},"s_9bac0f616d7bd3787076166f67d9":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5","e_c0f5eb4133131627838284c16b77"],"basis":"Stage 2 supplies the client's question about internal bleeding and confirms continued warfarin plus enoxaparin, framing the urgency triage the matrix scores."},"s_0ec5ca15f74b0296e396e4afbc1c":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"basis":"Stage 3 records only that the nurse reviewed emergency versus same-day clinic symptoms and that no new bruising is present, without assigning any listed sign to a category."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_7c305500bbb55a30839cfdc97da8","e_967323e828f8c1c84494d8299f0d","e_b30a6ce757e2af5a0292238fa33a","e_b4136754826b0d3963e88ce7e17c"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034","e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5","e_c0f5eb4133131627838284c16b77","e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"Stage 3 names the two response categories the item uses but never places intracranial, gastrointestinal, bruising, or epistaxis findings into either one, so the classification still requires clinical knowledge. No stage lists any of the four matrix rows. The Chinese stages mirror the English wording."}
{"rowToken":"r_178b4406ce755887ae37a79eebfe","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Whether day 19 findings represent inadequate sertraline response with obsessive-compulsive features requiring prescriber notification using score, function, and safety data.","stageEvaluations":{"s_909f2a7e09031c7e224d1ac588c1":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_a7db84b2aa0b899ef1d69d14ca5f"],"basis":"Stage 1's EPDS 20, 2-3 fragmented sleep hours, and not-yet-started therapy give the day-5 baseline against which day 19 is judged inadequate."},"s_42dfb9e97e83877a37130f9b30e3":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_f2dad047d62f12ce8ac52c460613","e_5e7089ce666dc1410c8239c0d375"],"basis":"Stage 2 supplies PHQ-9 16, EPDS 18, new choking imagery, 15-20 minute breathing checks, bathing and outing avoidance, and the client's dose question the cloze evaluates."},"s_783d9ae246cfff28aaf05e2c2a6e":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_19dc9d0499030e6590662eb1f4dc","e_f3749373647384070b270fe59ed1","e_a0337257416591e366615ef55627"],"basis":"The Stage 3 header states sertraline rose to 100 mg daily on day 21 with subsequent improvement, confirming that day 19 was an inadequate response escalated to the prescriber and that the drug was not stopped."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_f99de6b464ce1c54f695aa001eb3","e_915554193ba094d5b45328b35a1f","e_5c4c923c12f4e5234dcdc64951bd","e_1cf5aaeb4887fcded84ab54c50a4"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_a7db84b2aa0b899ef1d69d14ca5f","e_f2dad047d62f12ce8ac52c460613","e_5e7089ce666dc1410c8239c0d375","e_19dc9d0499030e6590662eb1f4dc","e_f3749373647384070b270fe59ed1","e_a0337257416591e366615ef55627"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_783d9ae246cfff28aaf05e2c2a6e","evidenceIds":["e_19dc9d0499030e6590662eb1f4dc","e_f3749373647384070b270fe59ed1"],"verbatimSpan":"dose 100 mg daily since day 21"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The visible day-33 stage discloses that the dose was doubled two days after the day-19 visit and that symptoms then eased, which retroactively answers the first blank as inadequate response and makes prescriber-directed escalation the obvious second blank. It also eliminates the reassurance and stop-the-drug distractors. The Chinese stage title carries the same dose-change disclosure."}
{"rowToken":"r_0400471b834d5a761fa4d97ae989","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"Whether the 1100 rapid response handoff must convey the unresolved Full Code status, refusal of intubation, assessed capacity, and escalation history.","stageEvaluations":{"s_d8b7d99653e7ab7e01b888782ca0":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_bbb9276e7f1ecfdd391f2fd99837"],"basis":"Stage 1 documents the verbatim refusal of tube, ventilator, and CPR, the capacity charting, and that code status stayed Full Code after both physicians deferred."},"s_a9e24816c6407b7d9a0118d9afa4":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_441ab19e92bf05f181b0e5656aad"],"basis":"Stage 2 supplies the 0950 page about respiratory failure, the open-ended 'as soon as I can' reply, and the repeated bedside refusal that make up the escalation history to relay."},"s_57495af5854daa284b9870671304":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_8d1a9c2b393cd317739235fc0051"],"basis":"Stage 3 reports that Dr. Anand questioned Mr. Jeffries directly and entered DNAR/DNI, an outcome that never states which elements the nurse included in the handoff."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_dce3eb04b3e78120146d75ada447","e_f9b3c31b8d6f9a9000e24e3de169","e_53de19e907dc102a67c2ba3a4901","e_c05791300263134551fd1800afd4"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_bbb9276e7f1ecfdd391f2fd99837","e_441ab19e92bf05f181b0e5656aad","e_8d1a9c2b393cd317739235fc0051"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The stages establish the code-status conflict and paging history the handoff must carry but never enumerate handoff content, and the DNAR/DNI entry is downstream resolution rather than a statement of what the nurse said. The sedation and stand-down distractors fail on the client's documented refusal and need for provider evaluation, not on stage text. Both languages narrate the same sequence."}
{"rowToken":"r_5a5df4f243a348d9837a7818a70b","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"Whether the nurse teaches consistent daily vitamin K intake rather than endorsing elimination, daily spinach loading, or INR-driven vegetable timing.","stageEvaluations":{"s_052e75edc6951d7909f90842a32a":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034"],"basis":"Stage 1 records the wife buying a large bag of frozen spinach and kale to 'build his blood back up,' the intake-increase and anemia misconception the teaching must correct."},"s_9bac0f616d7bd3787076166f67d9":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5"],"basis":"Stage 2's ibuprofen discontinuation, acetaminophen order, and injection return demonstration have no bearing on vitamin K counseling."},"s_0ec5ca15f74b0296e396e4afbc1c":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"basis":"Stage 3 contains the client's green-vegetable question and the wife's printed avoid-all-leafy-greens list, the two dietary plans the nurse must address."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_ce3ab5990eb00d7d51eb47c30bb4","e_84b51581dd64d1311f792d9ff58b","e_a2ea49a36bb5e719dc76a54746fd","e_64f105e857ac138bb7b8d766949f"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_3c6e5e9aa32aff7a2654c398e034","e_f8a93f5cb2e4d6ea7b59c60380e5","e_9f5cbf021eb447a744f052a168bf"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"No stage states the consistency principle or corrects the internet avoid list; the record only presents the two opposite misconceptions the nurse must reconcile. Stage 2's injection and analgesic content is unrelated to diet. The Chinese stages present the same spinach purchase and printed list."}
{"rowToken":"r_8981c6a964ede40dba88a3e8e3f5","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Whether anticipated naloxone is given IV in small repeated titrated doses aimed at restoring ventilation and arousability rather than eliminating analgesia.","stageEvaluations":{"s_40ff4cf1825867d9825ca6a4c63b":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_5b457a9235ad1faffb928018ec44"],"basis":"The 0830 findings of RR 8, SpO2 89%, 2 mm pupils, and POSS 3 after three morphine doses establish the opioid-induced depression that makes reversal anticipated."},"s_f640768ccd6e9b3c33f3a096b8e7":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_d55f2bec1d139ad6df15ceadc029"],"basis":"The 0838 update shows stimulation, repositioning, and 2 L oxygen failing with 6-8 second apneic pauses and notes naloxone stocked on the unit, setting up the reversal decision."},"s_3632511503308c2f103a5d57e7c5":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_a53d83fdb3dede1b1b280cbfe0d8","e_af9f8dcd51978369c8afa8c06387"],"basis":"The 0855 note states two 0.04 mg IV naloxone doses three minutes apart followed by spontaneous eye opening, command following, and deeper breaths, supplying the route, the small repeated dosing, and the ventilation/arousal endpoint."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_d1b4932b766038eeb828e8a9ae7a","e_7a766abbab830e50a06137740a3c","e_4aad7fe55364d1a125a386d6051b","e_310e1d180ced85edf9a286ebb33c"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_5b457a9235ad1faffb928018ec44","e_d55f2bec1d139ad6df15ceadc029","e_a53d83fdb3dede1b1b280cbfe0d8","e_af9f8dcd51978369c8afa8c06387"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_3632511503308c2f103a5d57e7c5","evidenceIds":["e_a53d83fdb3dede1b1b280cbfe0d8","e_af9f8dcd51978369c8afa8c06387"],"verbatimSpan":"Naloxone 0.04 mg IV was administered at 0843 per protocol/order, with a second 0.04 mg IV dose at 0846 for partial response."},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"All three cloze blanks are filled by the visible 0855 stage, which shows the IV route, incremental repeated 0.04 mg dosing, and improvement measured as arousal and deeper breathing. Nothing further is required for the candidate to complete the anticipatory statement. The Chinese stage repeats the same dosing narrative."}
{"rowToken":"r_e446f245491b5289e947fa799a63","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which mitigation and reporting actions belong in the response to the five morning privacy events, and that incident reports stay out of the chart.","stageEvaluations":{"s_a8457ab444ffb3e32bc9c86a405a":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_39238f23d5a551603f0d7b24e40b"],"basis":"Stage 1 supplies the five events the actions must address: the unauthorized husband caller, RN Casey's snooping, the unsigned employer fax, the student photo, and the hallway report."},"s_0d9202a772bf4deff99461527985":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_a2c65244071c607f52539b7a8adf","e_49b9b3f29c06bb3a372ab391b042"],"basis":"Stage 2 narrates each keyed action outright, including securing the fax for HIM, supervised photo deletion, moving the hallway report, withholding information from Robert, and completing reporting that is not documented in the medical record, which retires the sole wrong option."},"s_497c410545651ade56fa8ff236d5":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_2f12973b9321444c2de3c4460d9a"],"basis":"Stage 3's Privacy Officer investigation, fax signage, and in-service safeguards evaluate the outcome rather than name the immediate morning response actions."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_ff39d3628079707192441e0eb227","e_4e364aaf265f9e8fb9b5df0d55de","e_e2e8fb87d154141becfd01828869","e_6c291055edf28ec6fb829e85654a"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_39238f23d5a551603f0d7b24e40b","e_a2c65244071c607f52539b7a8adf","e_49b9b3f29c06bb3a372ab391b042","e_2f12973b9321444c2de3c4460d9a"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_0d9202a772bf4deff99461527985","evidenceIds":["e_a2c65244071c607f52539b7a8adf","e_49b9b3f29c06bb3a372ab391b042"],"verbatimSpan":"By 1200, incident reporting is complete and is not documented in the medical record."},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"Stage 2 performs and records every keyed action in the same order as the options and explicitly states the incident report was kept out of the medical record, which eliminates the only incorrect choice. The select-all becomes a matching exercise against the visible narrative. The Chinese stage carries the identical statements."}
{"rowToken":"r_1a457e6155fb5302e07d9d5eca76","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"The safest sequence for a 22.4 mcg/mL trough: hold the dose, clarify with the prescriber, verify the MAR, administer, then document.","stageEvaluations":{"s_73a15e76a504d008b90407996b0c":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_99d2a3c95eb30a09718b94940d69"],"basis":"Stage 1's infusion reaction and the restart at half the original rate establish the premedication and slower-infusion precautions named in the administration step."},"s_0b7bc1ea20227034dd4ccb44bcdb":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_2aad52cb510aed0ddca9855880a7","e_9a9667ffccb586e7b024d4eca694"],"basis":"Stage 2 gives the supratherapeutic 22.4 mcg/mL trough, the imminent 1,250 mg dose, and the pharmacist's 1,000 mg recommendation still awaiting prescriber confirmation."},"s_91852bd94b1f7b105d342d697856":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_8143b0cdcd606065dc0192a22124"],"basis":"Day 4's creatinine 2.4, 180 mL overnight output, and new tinnitus follow the sequencing decision and confirm only that the 1,000 mg regimen was eventually used."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_62a3e5eb99a66bcfa6fbed0602eb","e_09af7d0e8d184d799d32be9359c3","e_a1c65192ac37393269be217066e4","e_ad4bba40feb64ae05e834e6ca53b"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_99d2a3c95eb30a09718b94940d69","e_2aad52cb510aed0ddca9855880a7","e_9a9667ffccb586e7b024d4eca694","e_8143b0cdcd606065dc0192a22124"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"No stage narrates the nurse holding the dose, calling the prescriber, verifying the MAR, or documenting, so the ordering must be reasoned from medication-safety principles. Stage 3's later disclosure that 1,000 mg every 12 hours was given confirms the endpoint but not the sequence of steps. The Chinese stages present the same trough and recommendation."}
{"rowToken":"r_7d6f3ffcfd3f8f2e0cac7ca6d7b1","verdict":"NO_LEAK_COMPLETE_RECORD","testedDecision":"Which 1130 loop-closing actions verify the DNAR/DNI order, visual code-status identifiers, proxy notification, and reporting of the delayed provider response.","stageEvaluations":{"s_d8b7d99653e7ab7e01b888782ca0":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_bbb9276e7f1ecfdd391f2fd99837"],"basis":"The 0745 attending unavailability until 1400 and Dr. Reeves's 1100 promise form the first half of the delay that justifies filing an incident report."},"s_a9e24816c6407b7d9a0118d9afa4":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_441ab19e92bf05f181b0e5656aad"],"basis":"The 0950 page during acute respiratory failure answered only with 'as soon as I can' completes the system-failure record that the incident-report option targets."},"s_57495af5854daa284b9870671304":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_8d1a9c2b393cd317739235fc0051","e_4725033760f87a8ce87601707b39"],"basis":"Stage 3 supplies the entered DNAR/DNI order and the daughter's completion of the advance directive, the items whose EHR entry and proxy notification must be verified at 1130."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_1549094f4725b808ab0549c08d7c","e_dc0c7be5a9b7533827c74880c803","e_c99b2c65bfcdefae3b5a8b598ce8","e_0554c49c5960fb40ba279226ebc2"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_bbb9276e7f1ecfdd391f2fd99837","e_441ab19e92bf05f181b0e5656aad","e_8d1a9c2b393cd317739235fc0051","e_4725033760f87a8ce87601707b39"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"This evaluation item deliberately spans the whole shift: the incident-report option is only defensible from the 0745 through 0950 delays, while the DNAR/DNI verification and proxy notification depend on the 1100-1130 events. Nothing in the record mentions wristband or door signage or rejects a retrospective note, so the wrong option survives on documentation principle alone. The Chinese stages track the same timeline."}
{"rowToken":"r_5fefc72e5b2011badd7854eea62f","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which Stage 2 colostomy findings are expected on postoperative day 3 and which — dusky stoma, absent output with cramping, skin breakdown — require escalation.","stageEvaluations":{"s_ce34cf88baf3428981ae154f287e":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_1ce3e940d46020450ea5dd2f05ba"],"basis":"Stage 1 addresses the missing qualified interpreter, the pending WOC consult, and the daughter guiding the patient's hands, none of which classify stoma or peristomal findings."},"s_76978f8cbecc6490c44052a53ba6":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_b084fcf380388b9caa8c199bc484","e_6ea0abb90ebb8c8c886a95de89fc"],"basis":"The Stage 2 teaching note lists the taught warning signs — dark purple or black stoma, peristomal breakdown, and no output beyond 6 hours with cramping — which are verbatim the three rows keyed as requiring escalation, while the same note reports the red moist stoma, intact skin, and looser output keyed as expected."},"s_cb8c7b16271425f7d4863a6ee277":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_a66b8227fd1983cceb89b85f7801"],"basis":"The discharge note repeats that the stoma remains red, moist, and functioning without categorizing any abnormal finding."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_44493c53eb3651694dd151fba975","e_d757e95a1656606bcd7d404fa09d","e_4942e9ffdf2d008beaf6b1db0e9d","e_5f6c5bfbb58627bf781204d1ffc0"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_1ce3e940d46020450ea5dd2f05ba","e_b084fcf380388b9caa8c199bc484","e_6ea0abb90ebb8c8c886a95de89fc","e_a66b8227fd1983cceb89b85f7801"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_76978f8cbecc6490c44052a53ba6","evidenceIds":["e_b084fcf380388b9caa8c199bc484","e_6ea0abb90ebb8c8c886a95de89fc"],"verbatimSpan":"three warning signs: stoma changes from red to dark purple or black, peristomal skin breakdown, and no output for more than 6 hours with cramping or distension"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The visible Stage 2 documentation pre-sorts the matrix: the three escalation rows are copied from the warning-sign list the patient verbalized, and the three expected rows are the same note's current assessment findings with stable vitals. A candidate can text-match rather than reason about stoma perfusion or obstruction. The Chinese exhibit lists the identical three warning signs."}
